{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_mmdft5osacs1y99lj4","content":"     1\t---\n     2\ttitle: Configuration Reference\n     3\tdescription: Complete reference for hank.json, hankweave.json, runtime settings, and environment variables\n     4\t---\n     5\t\n     6\t# Configuration Reference\n     7\t\n     8\tThis is a comprehensive reference for all configuration options in Hankweave. It covers the `hank.json` and `hankweave.json` files, runtime settings, and environment variables.\n     9\t\n    10\t## Who is this for?\n    11\t\n    12\tThis guide is for:\n    13\t\n    14\t- **Hank authors** (Track 2) who need detailed documentation for every configuration field.\n    15\t- **Developers building on Hankweave** (Track 3) who need to understand the complete configuration schema.\n    16\t\n    17\t## Configuration Files Overview\n    18\t\n    19\tHankweave's configuration lives in two JSON files.\n    20\t\n    21\t| File                           | Purpose                                                                               | Required |\n    22\t| ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n    23\t| `hank.json` (or `strand.json`) | Defines the hank itself—codons, loops, and sentinel attachments.                      | Yes      |\n    24\t| `hankweave.json`               | Defines runtime settings like the server port, model defaults, and sentinel behavior. | No       |\n    25\t\n    26\t![Configuration file structure](/images/concepts/config-structure.svg)\n    27\t\n    28\t## Hank File Structure\n    29\t\n    30\tA hank file is a JSON object with up to three top-level keys. Only `hank` is required.\n    31\t\n    32\t```json filename=\"hank.json\"\n    33\t{\n    34\t  \"meta\": {\n    35\t    \"name\": \"Data Codebook Generator\",\n    36\t    \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n    37\t    \"description\": \"Generates documented schemas from raw CSV files\",\n    38\t    \"author\": \"Your Name\"\n    39\t  },\n    40\t  \"overrides\": {\n    41\t    \"model\": \"sonnet\",\n    42\t    \"dataHashTimeLimit\": 10000,\n    43\t    \"sentinel\": {\n    44\t      \"enablePersistence\": true\n    45\t    }\n    46\t  },\n    47\t  \"hank\": [\n    48\t    {\n    49\t      /* codon or loop */\n    50\t    }\n    51\t  ]\n    52\t}\n    53\t```\n    54\t\n    55\t### Meta Section\n    56\t\n    57\tMetadata for humans. Add it when you're sharing hanks or want to remember what you built six months from now.\n    58\t\n    59\t| Field         | Type   | Description                        |\n    60\t| ------------- | ------ | ---------------------------------- |\n    61\t| `name`        | string | Human-readable name for the hank   |\n    62\t| `version`     | string | Semantic version (e.g., `\"1.0.0\"`) |\n    63\t| `description` | string | What this hank does                |\n    64\t| `author`      | string | Creator information                |\n    65\t\n    66\t### Overrides Section\n    67\t\n    68\tThe hank author's overrides for runtime settings. These values take precedence over the `hankweave.json` config file but can still be overridden by environment variables and CLI arguments.\n    69\t\n    70\t| Field               | Type   | Default | Description                                  |\n    71\t| ------------------- | ------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n    72\t| `model`             | string | —       | Preferred model for this hank                |\n    73\t| `dataHashTimeLimit` | number | 5000    | Time limit for hashing data directories (ms) |\n    74\t| `sentinel`          | object | —       | Sentinel system settings (see below)         |\n    75\t\n    76\t> **Configuration Layer Precedence**: Overrides are layer 3 of 5. They override built-in defaults and the runtime config file, but are themselves overridden by environment variables and CLI arguments. See [Configuration Layers](#configuration-layers) for the full hierarchy.\n    77\t\n    78\t### Requirements Section\n    79\t\n    80\tDeclare required environment variables for fail-fast validation at startup.\n    81\t\n    82\t| Field | Type     | Description                                        |\n    83\t| ----- | -------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n    84\t| `env` | string[] | Environment variable names required for this hank. |\n    85\t\n    86\t```json\n    87\t{\n    88\t  \"requirements\": {\n    89\t    \"env\": [\"ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\", \"DATABASE_URL\"]\n    90\t  }\n    91\t}\n    92\t```\n    93\t\n    94\tValidation runs during both `--validate` and normal startup. The `HANKWEAVE_` prefix is automatically supported: `HANKWEAVE_API_KEY` satisfies a requirement for `API_KEY`.\n    95\t\n    96\t### Global System Prompts\n    97\t\n    98\tApply a system prompt to ALL codons in the hank, useful for enforcing conventions across the entire workflow.\n    99\t\n   100\t| Field                    | Type               | Description                                             |\n   101\t| ------------------------ | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n   102\t| `globalSystemPromptFile` | string \\| string[] | Path(s) to system prompt file(s) applied to all codons. |\n   103\t| `globalSystemPromptText` | string             | Inline system prompt text applied to all codons.        |\n   104\t\n   105\t```json\n   106\t{\n   107\t  \"globalSystemPromptFile\": [\"./prompts/workspace-context.md\", \"./prompts/coding-standards.md\"],\n   108\t  \"hank\": [...]\n   109\t}\n   110\t```\n   111\t\n   112\tOr with inline text:\n   113\t\n   114\t```json\n   115\t{\n   116\t  \"globalSystemPromptText\": \"Always explain your reasoning. Never use TODO comments.\",\n   117\t  \"hank\": [...]\n   118\t}\n   119\t```\n   120\t\n   121\tThe global system prompt is prepended before any codon-specific `appendSystemPromptFile` or `appendSystemPromptText` content.\n   122\t\n   123\t**Common use cases for global prompts:**\n   124\t\n   125\t| Use Case            | Example Content                                                                                  |\n   126\t| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n   127\t| Workspace layout    | \"The codebase is a TypeScript monorepo with packages/ for libraries and apps/ for applications.\" |\n   128\t| Coding standards    | \"Use functional components. All functions need JSDoc. No console.log in production.\"             |\n   129\t| Domain context      | \"This is a fintech app. PII must never be logged. All amounts are in cents.\"                     |\n   130\t| Project constraints | \"We support Node 18+. Don't use features from Node 20.\"                                          |\n   131\t\n   132\tUsing global prompts avoids repetition and ensures consistency—change the rules in one place, every codon sees the update.\n   133\t\n   134\t### Hank Array\n   135\t\n   136\tThe `hank` array is the actual program—your codons and loops in execution order. Everything else is metadata and settings; this is where the work happens.\n   137\t\n   138\t```json\n   139\t{\n   140\t  \"hank\": [\n   141\t    {\n   142\t      /* codon 1 */\n   143\t    },\n   144\t    {\n   145\t      /* codon 2 */\n   146\t    },\n   147\t    { \"type\": \"loop\" /* loop definition */ },\n   148\t    {\n   149\t      /* codon 3 */\n   150\t    }\n   151\t  ]\n   152\t}\n   153\t```\n   154\t\n   155\tItems execute sequentially. See [Codon Configuration](#codon-configuration) and [Loop Configuration](#loop-configuration) for field details.\n   156\t\n   157\t---\n   158\t\n   159\t## Codon Configuration\n   160\t\n   161\tA codon is the atomic unit of work—a single agent task with its own prompt, model, and settings. If hanks are programs, codons are the statements.\n   162\t\n   163\t### Required Fields\n   164\t\n   165\tEvery codon must have these fields:\n   166\t\n   167\t| Field              | Type                               | Description                                                                                                           |\n   168\t| ------------------ | ---------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   169\t| `id`               | string                             | Unique identifier (e.g., `\"schema-gen\"`, `\"validate-output\"`)                                                         |\n   170\t| `name`             | string                             | Human-readable name shown in UI and logs                                                                              |\n   171\t| `model`            | string                             | Model identifier for the LLM. See [Model Specification](#model-specification).                                        |\n   172\t| `continuationMode` | `\"fresh\"` \\| `\"continue-previous\"` | Specifies how to handle context. `\"fresh\"` starts a new conversation; `\"continue-previous\"` adds to the existing one. |\n   173\t\n   174\tPlus **one of**:\n   175\t\n   176\t- `promptFile`: Path(s) to prompt file(s)\n   177\t- `promptText`: Inline prompt string\n   178\t\n   179\t### Complete Field Reference\n   180\t\n   181\tHere's a codon with every possible field. You'll rarely need all of these—most codons are much simpler.\n   182\t\n   183\t```json filename=\"codon-full-example.json\"\n   184\t{\n   185\t  \"type\": \"codon\",\n   186\t  \"id\": \"generate-schema\",\n   187\t  \"name\": \"Schema Generation\",\n   188\t  \"description\": \"Generates Zod schemas from CSV observations\",\n   189\t\n   190\t  \"model\": \"sonnet\",\n   191\t  \"continuationMode\": \"continue-previous\",\n   192\t\n   193\t  \"promptFile\": [\"./prompts/schema-gen.md\"],\n   194\t  \"promptText\": \"Alternative: inline prompt text\",\n   195\t\n   196\t  \"appendSystemPromptFile\": [\"./system/rules.md\"],\n   197\t  \"appendSystemPromptText\": \"Alternative: inline system prompt\",\n   198\t\n   199\t  \"rigSetup\": [\n   200\t    {\n"}]},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:28:32.915Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_mmdft9g0qdxftwe1ov","content":"     1\t---\n     2\ttitle: Files and Directories\n     3\tdescription: Complete guide to Hankweave's file layout - execution directories, state files, logs, checkpoints, and outputs\n     4\t---\n     5\t\n     6\t# Files and Directories\n     7\t\n     8\tWhen you run a hank, Hankweave creates a self-contained **execution directory** for all its work, including state files, logs, checkpoints, and agent outputs. This guide maps out that entire structure. Understanding it is key for debugging, building custom tools, or simply seeing what's happening under the hood.\n     9\t\n    10\tThis page details every file and directory Hankweave creates, with enough detail that you could recreate the structure from scratch.\n    11\t\n    12\timport { Callout } from \"nextra/components\";\n    13\t\n    14\t<Callout type=\"info\">\n    15\t  **Who is this for?** This guide is for: - **Hank Users**: Finding logs,\n    16\t  outputs, and checkpoints when debugging. - **Tool Builders**: Understanding\n    17\t  which files to read when building on Hankweave. - **Hankweave Contributors**:\n    18\t  Knowing the full file layout for development.\n    19\t</Callout>\n    20\t\n    21\t## Execution Directory Structure\n    22\t\n    23\tHankweave operates inside an **execution directory**. This workspace contains all generated files: agent outputs, state, logs, and checkpoints. Your original data is kept separate and untouched, accessed via a read-only symlink within this directory.\n    24\t\n    25\t![Execution Environment](/images/concepts/execution-environment.svg)\n    26\t\n    27\tThe full structure looks like this:\n    28\t\n    29\t```plaintext\n    30\t~/.hankweave-executions/{timestamp}-{random}-{hash}/    # Default location\n    31\t├── read_only_data_source/                              # Data access (symlink or copy)\n    32\t│   └── {your data files}\n    33\t├── agentRoot/                                          # Agent working directory\n    34\t│   ├── notes/                                          # Files created by agents\n    35\t│   ├── code/\n    36\t│   └── ...\n    37\t├── rigArchive/                                         # Archived rig setups (optional)\n    38\t│   └── {codonId}/                                      # Archived after successful codon\n    39\t│       └── {copied files from rig}\n    40\t└── .hankweave/                                         # Hankweave internal state\n    41\t    ├── runtime.lock                                    # Server PID + heartbeat + port\n    42\t    ├── execution-meta.json                             # Execution metadata\n    43\t    ├── state.json                                      # Current state\n    44\t    ├── state.json.bak                                  # Backup state\n    45\t    ├── archive-manifest.json                           # Tracks all archived files\n    46\t    ├── events/\n    47\t    │   └── events.jsonl                                # Event journal\n    48\t    ├── logs/\n    49\t    │   ├── server.log                                  # Server logs\n    50\t    │   └── websocket.log                               # WebSocket traffic\n    51\t    ├── runs/\n    52\t    │   └── {runId}/\n    53\t    │       ├── {codonId}-claude.log                    # Per-codon agent logs\n    54\t    │       └── ...\n    55\t    ├── checkpoints/\n    56\t    │   ├── .hankweavecheckpoints/                      # Shadow git repository\n    57\t    │   └── .gitconfig                                  # Isolated git config\n    58\t    └── sentinels/\n    59\t        ├── outputs/\n    60\t        │   └── {sentinelId}/\n    61\t        │       ├── {id}-{codon}-{timestamp}.md         # Text outputs\n    62\t        │       └── {id}-{codon}-{timestamp}.ndjson     # Structured outputs\n    63\t        └── history/\n    64\t            └── {sentinelId}-codon-{codonId}.json       # Conversational history\n    65\t```\n    66\t\n    67\t### Execution Directory Location\n    68\t\n    69\tBy default, Hankweave creates execution directories in `~/.hankweave-executions/`. Directory names use the format:\n    70\t\n    71\t```\n    72\t{timestamp}-{random}-{dataHash}\n    73\t```\n    74\t\n    75\t- **timestamp**: Unix timestamp in milliseconds (e.g., `1736000000000`)\n    76\t- **random**: 4-character random string (e.g., `x7a2`)\n    77\t- **dataHash**: First 6 characters of your data source hash (e.g., `abc123`)\n    78\t\n    79\tYou can specify a custom location with `--execution=/path/to/dir`.\n    80\t\n    81\t<Callout type=\"warning\">\n    82\t  **Reserved Location**: The `~/.hankweave-executions/` directory is managed by\n    83\t  Hankweave. Do not use the `--execution` flag to point to a path inside this\n    84\t  directory. If you need a custom location, choose one elsewhere on your\n    85\t  filesystem.\n    86\t</Callout>\n    87\t\n    88\t### read_only_data_source\n    89\t\n    90\tThis directory provides read-only access to your original data. By default, it's a **symlink** to your data source, isolating the execution while providing necessary file access to agents.\n    91\t\n    92\tWith `--copy`, Hankweave copies your data instead of symlinking. Use this when:\n    93\t\n    94\t- Your data source might change during execution.\n    95\t- You need the execution directory to be fully self-contained.\n    96\t- Symlinks aren't supported (e.g., some CI environments).\n    97\t\n    98\tThe `read_only_data_source` directory is **never checkpointed**. This is enforced at the file resolver level; the `UnifiedFileResolver` always ignores this path, regardless of your `checkpointedFiles` patterns.\n    99\t\n   100\t### agentRoot/\n   101\t\n   102\tThe `agentRoot/` directory is where agents do their work. This is the working directory passed to agent processes, and all file operations happen here. Everything the agent creates—code, documentation, analysis results—lives in this directory.\n   103\t\n   104\tFiles in `agentRoot/` **are** tracked by checkpoints (if they match `checkpointedFiles` patterns), allowing you to roll back the workspace to any previous state.\n   105\t\n   106\t### rigArchive/\n   107\t\n   108\tWhen you set `archiveOnSuccess` on a codon (as an array of glob patterns), Hankweave moves matching files here after a successful codon completion. This keeps `agentRoot/` clean between iterations while preserving the rig's artifacts for debugging.\n   109\t\n   110\t```json\n   111\t{\n   112\t  \"rigSetup\": [\n   113\t    {\n   114\t      \"type\": \"copy\",\n   115\t      \"copy\": { \"from\": \"./templates\", \"to\": \"src\" }\n   116\t    }\n   117\t  ],\n   118\t  \"archiveOnSuccess\": [\"src/**/*\"]\n   119\t}\n   120\t```\n   121\t\n   122\tAfter the codon completes successfully, files matching the `archiveOnSuccess` patterns are moved to `rigArchive/{codonId}/`.\n   123\t\n   124\t<Callout type=\"info\">\n   125\t  **When to use archiving**: Use `archiveOnSuccess` when large template\n   126\t  directories would otherwise accumulate across loop iterations. This keeps your\n   127\t  working directory lean while preserving artifacts for debugging.\n   128\t</Callout>\n   129\t\n   130\t## The .hankweave Directory\n   131\t\n   132\tAll internal state lives here. This directory contains everything Hankweave needs to track execution, persist events, and enable time-travel debugging.\n   133\t\n   134\t### runtime.lock\n   135\t\n   136\tThis file tracks the running server process to prevent conflicts and detect crashes. It answers two questions: is a server running in this directory, and is it still alive?\n   137\t\n   138\t```json filename=\"runtime.lock\"\n   139\t{\n   140\t  \"pid\": 12345,\n   141\t  \"runId\": \"1736000000000-abc123-def456\",\n   142\t  \"startTime\": \"2025-01-04T12:00:00.000Z\",\n   143\t  \"lastHeartbeat\": \"2025-01-04T12:01:30.000Z\",\n   144\t  \"port\": 54321\n   145\t}\n   146\t```\n   147\t\n   148\t| Field           | Description                                                              |\n   149\t| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n   150\t| `pid`           | Process ID of the running server                                         |\n   151\t| `runId`         | Current run identifier                                                   |\n   152\t| `startTime`     | When the server started                                                  |\n   153\t| `lastHeartbeat` | Timestamp updated every 30 seconds                                       |\n   154\t| `port`          | The WebSocket server port (useful for `--attach` and external tooling)  |\n   155\t\n   156\tThis enables **crash detection**. If a new server starts and finds a lock file with a stale heartbeat, it can infer that the previous process crashed and initiate recovery.\n   157\t\n   158\t### execution-meta.json\n   159\t\n   160\tThis file captures the execution environment's identity—where it came from, what it's working with, and how it connects to your data.\n   161\t\n   162\t```json filename=\"execution-meta.json\"\n   163\t{\n   164\t  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n   165\t  \"readOnlySourceDataPath\": \"/path/to/your/data\",\n   166\t  \"readOnlySourceResolvedDataPath\": \"/actual/resolved/path\",\n   167\t  \"dataHash\": \"abc123def456...\",\n   168\t  \"hankHash\": \"789xyz...\",\n   169\t  \"hankPath\": \"/path/to/hank.json\",\n   170\t  \"linkType\": \"symlink\",\n   171\t  \"createdAt\": \"2025-01-04T12:00:00.000Z\",\n   172\t  \"lastUsed\": \"2025-01-04T14:30:00.000Z\"\n   173\t}\n   174\t```\n   175\t\n   176\t| Field                            | Description                                                |\n   177\t| -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n   178\t| `version`                        | Metadata format version                                    |\n   179\t| `readOnlySourceDataPath`         | The original path to your data source, as you provided it. |\n   180\t| `readOnlySourceResolvedDataPath` | The absolute, canonical path after resolving any symlinks. |\n   181\t| `dataHash`                       | Hash of your data source (for resume detection).           |\n   182\t| `hankHash`                       | Hash of `hank.json` content (to detect config changes).    |\n   183\t| `hankPath`                       | Path to the hank configuration file.                       |\n   184\t| `linkType`                       | How data was linked: `symlink` or `copy`.                  |\n   185\t| `createdAt`                      | When this execution directory was created.                 |\n   186\t| `lastUsed`                       | Last time this execution was used.                         |\n   187\t\n   188\tThe `dataHash` is how Hankweave finds existing executions. If you run `hankweave --data=/my/project` twice, the second run will resume in the same execution directory because the hash matches.\n   189\t\n   190\tThe `hankHash` tracks config changes. If you modify your `hank.json` between runs, Hankweave will warn you that the configuration has changed.\n   191\t\n   192\t### state.json\n   193\t\n   194\tThe heart of Hankweave's persistence layer. This file contains the complete execution history: runs, codons, checkpoints, and costs. See [State File Reference](/reference/state-file) for the full schema.\n   195\t\n   196\tA backup (`state.json.bak`) is created before each save. If the primary file is corrupted, Hankweave automatically falls back to the backup.\n   197\t\n   198\t### events/events.jsonl\n   199\t\n   200\tThe persistent event journal. Every significant event is appended here as a single JSON line, creating a complete, ordered audit trail.\n   201\t\n   202\t```jsonl filename=\"events.jsonl\"\n   203\t{\"type\":\"codon.started\",\"timestamp\":\"2025-01-04T12:00:01.000Z\",\"data\":{\"codonId\":\"observe#0\",...}}\n   204\t{\"type\":\"assistant.action\",\"timestamp\":\"2025-01-04T12:00:05.000Z\",\"data\":{\"action\":\"tool_use\",...}}\n   205\t{\"type\":\"file.updated\",\"timestamp\":\"2025-01-04T12:00:10.000Z\",\"data\":{\"path\":\"notes/observations.md\",...}}\n   206\t{\"type\":\"codon.completed\",\"timestamp\":\"2025-01-04T12:01:00.000Z\",\"data\":{\"codonId\":\"observe#0\",\"success\":true,...}}\n   207\t```\n   208\t\n   209\tSee [Event Journal](/reference/event-journal) for the complete schema of journaled events.\n   210\t\n   211\t### logs/\n   212\t\n   213\tTwo log files reside here, each for a different purpose.\n   214\t\n   215\t**server.log** captures human-readable server activity:\n   216\t\n   217\t```plaintext filename=\"server.log\"\n   218\t2025-01-04T12:00:00.000Z [INFO] Starting HankweaveRuntime on port 54321\n   219\t2025-01-04T12:00:01.000Z [INFO] Beginning codon: observe#0\n   220\t2025-01-04T12:00:05.000Z [DEBUG] File change detected: agentRoot/notes/observations.md\n"}]},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:28:32.917Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_mmdften8p48jllg1tm","content":"{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://biomejs.dev/schemas/2.0.6/schema.json\",\n  \"files\": {\n    \"includes\": [\n      \"server/**/*\",\n      \"!server/llm/*.json\",\n      \"tests/e2e/**/*\",\n      \"tests/utils/**/*\",\n      \"tests/unit/**/*\",\n      \"types/**/*\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"linter\": {\n    \"enabled\": true,\n    \"rules\": {\n      \"recommended\": true,\n      \"complexity\": {\n        \"noBannedTypes\": \"off\"\n      },\n      \"style\": {\n        \"noParameterAssign\": \"off\",\n        \"useConst\": \"warn\"\n      },\n      \"suspicious\": {\n        \"noExplicitAny\": \"warn\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"formatter\": {\n    \"enabled\": true,\n    \"formatWithErrors\": false,\n    \"indentStyle\": \"space\",\n    \"indentWidth\": 2,\n    \"lineWidth\": 100,\n    \"lineEnding\": \"lf\"\n  }\n}\n"}]},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:28:32.917Z"}
