{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_mmdfy1envoi271k1rt","content":"     1\t# Meet Hankweave \n     2\t\n     3\t\n     4\tThe official documentation for Hankweave—a runtime for reliable, brownfield AI engineering.\n     5\t\n     6\tThese docs were built by Hankweave. So were the diagrams. \n     7\t\n     8\tWe're eating our own cooking, and soon, you will too. \n     9\t\n    10\t---\n    11\t\n    12\t## What is Hankweave?\n    13\t\n    14\tHankweave freezes ephemeral agentic behaviors into declarative, reproducible programs. When your agent fails on run #47, you don't debug \"emergent behavior.\" You trace the exact sequence of events, roll back to a checkpoint, and fix it.\n    15\t\n    16\tThe core abstraction is the **codon**—an atomic unit of agent work with a prompt, a model, and tracked files. Codons sequence into **hanks** (JSON programs). Between codons, you get git checkpoints, event logs, and clear boundaries. Problems in codon 3 don't leak into codon 7.\n    17\t\n    18\tFor the full picture, start with [Codons](/concepts/codons) and [Hanks](/concepts/hanks).\n    19\t\n    20\t---\n    21\t\n    22\t## Reading the Docs\n    23\t\n    24\tThe documentation is organized into four sections:\n    25\t\n    26\t| Section | What You'll Find |\n    27\t|---------|------------------|\n    28\t| **Guides** | Step-by-step tutorials—start here if you're new |\n    29\t| **Concepts** | How Hankweave works: codons, hanks, loops, checkpoints, sentinels |\n    30\t| **Reference** | CLI, WebSocket protocol, configuration, state machine specs |\n    31\t| **Use Cases** | Production patterns and real-world examples |\n    32\t\n    33\t### Recommended Reading Order\n    34\t\n    35\t**Just getting started (Running Hanks):**\n    36\t\n    37\t1. [Getting Started](/guides/getting-started) — Install and run your first hank\n    38\t2. [Codons](/concepts/codons) — The atomic unit of agentic work\n    39\t3. [Hanks](/concepts/hanks) — How codons compose into programs\n    40\t\n    41\t**Building Hanks:**\n    42\t\n    43\t- [Building a Hank](/guides/building-a-hank) — The CCEPL development loop\n    44\t- [Loops](/concepts/loops), [Rigs](/concepts/rigs), [Sentinels](/concepts/sentinels) — Advanced constructs\n    45\t\n    46\t**Debugging Hanks:**\n    47\t\n    48\t- [Debugging](/guides/debugging) — Every tool call is logged. The answer is in there.\n    49\t\n    50\t**Building on top of Hanks:**\n    51\t\n    52\t- [WebSocket Protocol](/reference/protocol) and [Client Libraries](/reference/client-libraries)\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\t---\n    56\t\n    57\t## About Southbridge\n    58\t\n    59\tHankweave is built by [Southbridge](https://www.southbridge.ai). \n    60\t\n    61\tWe build infrastructure for AI systems that need to keep working six months from now—the infra for reliable, brownfield AI engineering. \n    62\t\n    63\tCheck out our website - maybe start with: \n    64\t- [CCEPL-Driven Development](https://www.southbridge.ai/blog/ccepl-driven-development)\n    65\t- [Antibrittle Agents](https://www.southbridge.ai/blog/antibrittle-agents)\n"}]},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:28:32.917Z"}
